## What Auto-Configuration Actually Is

Spring Boot auto-configuration eliminates the boilerplate XML and Java configuration you'd write in traditional Spring. Instead of manually configuring DataSource, EntityManagerFactory, TransactionManager, etc., Spring Boot does it automatically when it detects the right dependencies.

**The problem it solves:**
```java
// Traditional Spring - you had to configure all this
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        DriverManagerDataSource ds = new DriverManagerDataSource();
        ds.setDriverClassName("org.h2.Driver");
        ds.setUrl("jdbc:h2:mem:testdb");
        return ds;
    }

    @Bean
    public LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean entityManagerFactory() {
        // 20+ lines of configuration
    }

    @Bean
    public PlatformTransactionManager transactionManager() {
        // More configuration
    }
}
```

**Spring Boot - zero configuration:**
```java
// Just add dependency in pom.xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
</dependency>

// Add properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb

// Done! All beans auto-configured
```

## How It Works - The Complete Flow

### Step 1: @SpringBootApplication Enables Auto-Configuration

```java
@SpringBootApplication  // This is the trigger
public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }
}
```

`@SpringBootApplication` is composed of three annotations:
```java
@SpringBootConfiguration  // Same as @Configuration
@EnableAutoConfiguration  // THIS is the key
@ComponentScan
public @interface SpringBootApplication { }
```

### Step 2: @EnableAutoConfiguration Imports Configuration

```java
@Import(AutoConfigurationImportSelector.class)
public @interface EnableAutoConfiguration { }
```

`AutoConfigurationImportSelector` is the engine that:
1. Reads auto-configuration class names from files
2. Loads those configuration classes
3. Evaluates their conditions
4. Registers beans if conditions match

### Step 3: Reading Auto-Configuration Classes

**Spring Boot 3.x** reads from:
```
META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports
```

**Spring Boot 2.x** reads from:
```
META-INF/spring.factories
```

Example `AutoConfiguration.imports` file:
```
org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceAutoConfiguration
org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.orm.jpa.HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration
org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.web.servlet.WebMvcAutoConfiguration
org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.security.servlet.SecurityAutoConfiguration
```

### Step 4: Conditional Evaluation

Each auto-configuration class has conditions that must be met:

```java
@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass({ DataSource.class, EmbeddedDatabaseType.class })
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(type = "io.r2dbc.spi.ConnectionFactory")
@EnableConfigurationProperties(DataSourceProperties.class)
@Import({ DataSourcePoolMetadataProvidersConfiguration.class })
public class DataSourceAutoConfiguration {

    @Configuration
    @Conditional(EmbeddedDatabaseCondition.class)
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean({ DataSource.class, XADataSource.class })
    @Import(EmbeddedDataSourceConfiguration.class)
    protected static class EmbeddedDatabaseConfiguration { }

    @Configuration
    @ConditionalOnClass(HikariDataSource.class)
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class)
    @ConditionalOnProperty(name = "spring.datasource.type", 
                           havingValue = "com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource", 
                           matchIfMissing = true)
    static class Hikari {
        @Bean
        @ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "spring.datasource.hikari")
        HikariDataSource dataSource(DataSourceProperties properties) {
            HikariDataSource dataSource = createDataSource(
                properties, HikariDataSource.class
            );
            return dataSource;
        }
    }
}
```

**Conditions explained:**
- `@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)` - Only activate if DataSource class is on classpath (meaning JDBC dependency is present)
- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class)` - Only create DataSource if user hasn't already defined one
- `@ConditionalOnProperty` - Only activate if specific property is set

## Real-World Example: Adding Database Support

**Step 1: Add dependency**
```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
    <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

**Step 2: Add properties**
```properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop
spring.jpa.show-sql=true
```

**What gets auto-configured automatically:**
1. ✅ **DataSource** (HikariCP connection pool)
2. ✅ **EntityManagerFactory** (JPA)
3. ✅ **TransactionManager** (JPA transactions)
4. ✅ **JpaRepositories** (Spring Data JPA)
5. ✅ **Hibernate** as JPA provider
6. ✅ **Database initialization** (schema.sql, data.sql)

**You can now just use it:**
```java
@Entity
public class User {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;
    private String name;
}

@Repository
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    // Auto-configured and ready to use!
}

@Service
public class UserService {
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;  // Works automatically

    @Transactional  // Transaction manager auto-configured
    public User save(User user) {
        return userRepository.save(user);
    }
}
```

## Key Conditional Annotations

### @ConditionalOnClass
Activates when specific class is present on classpath.
```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnClass(RedisTemplate.class)  // Only if Redis client is present
public class RedisConfig {
    @Bean
    public RedisTemplate<String, Object> redisTemplate() {
        // Configure Redis
    }
}
```

### @ConditionalOnMissingBean
Creates bean only if user hasn't defined it. **This is why you can override auto-configuration.**
```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnMissingBean
public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    return new RestTemplate();  // Default config
}

// Your custom config overrides it
@Configuration
public class MyConfig {
    @Bean
    public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
        RestTemplate rt = new RestTemplate();
        rt.setRequestFactory(new HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory());
        return rt;  // This wins, auto-config backs off
    }
}
```

### @ConditionalOnProperty
Activates based on application properties.
```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.feature.enabled", havingValue = "true")
public class FeatureConfig {
    // Only loads if app.feature.enabled=true
}
```

### @ConditionalOnWebApplication
Activates only in web applications.
```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnWebApplication(type = Type.SERVLET)
public class WebMvcConfig {
    // Only in servlet-based web apps
}
```

## Viewing What Got Auto-Configured

**Enable debug mode:**
```properties
debug=true
```

**Or run with:**
```bash
java -jar app.jar --debug
```

**Output shows:**
```
Positive matches:
-----------------
DataSourceAutoConfiguration matched:
  - @ConditionalOnClass found required classes 'javax.sql.DataSource', 'org.springframework.jdbc.datasource.embedded.EmbeddedDatabaseType' (OnClassCondition)

JpaRepositoriesAutoConfiguration matched:
  - @ConditionalOnClass found required class 'org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository' (OnClassCondition)
  - @ConditionalOnMissingBean (types: org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.support.JpaRepositoryFactoryBean; SearchStrategy: all) did not find any beans (OnBeanCondition)

Negative matches:
-----------------
MongoAutoConfiguration:
  Did not match:
    - @ConditionalOnClass did not find required class 'com.mongodb.client.MongoClient' (OnClassCondition)

RedisAutoConfiguration:
  Did not match:
    - @ConditionalOnClass did not find required class 'org.springframework.data.redis.core.RedisOperations' (OnClassCondition)
```

## Disabling Specific Auto-Configurations

**Method 1: In @SpringBootApplication**
```java
@SpringBootApplication(exclude = {DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class})
public class Application { }
```

**Method 2: In application.properties**
```properties
spring.autoconfigure.exclude=org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceAutoConfiguration
```

**Method 3: Using @EnableAutoConfiguration**
```java
@Configuration
@EnableAutoConfiguration(exclude = {SecurityAutoConfiguration.class})
public class Config { }
```

## Common Auto-Configurations and Their Triggers

| Starter Dependency | Auto-Configuration | What Gets Configured |
|-------------------|-------------------|---------------------|
| spring-boot-starter-web | WebMvcAutoConfiguration | Tomcat, DispatcherServlet, Jackson, static resources |
| spring-boot-starter-data-jpa | HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration | DataSource, EntityManager, TransactionManager |
| spring-boot-starter-security | SecurityAutoConfiguration | SecurityFilterChain, default login page |
| spring-boot-starter-data-redis | RedisAutoConfiguration | RedisTemplate, connection factory |
| spring-boot-starter-actuator | ActuatorAutoConfiguration | Health, metrics, info endpoints |

## Creating Custom Auto-Configuration

```java
// 1. Create auto-configuration class
@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass(MyLibraryClient.class)
@EnableConfigurationProperties(MyLibraryProperties.class)
public class MyLibraryAutoConfiguration {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public MyLibraryClient myLibraryClient(MyLibraryProperties properties) {
        return new MyLibraryClient(properties.getApiKey());
    }
}

// 2. Create properties
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "mylibrary")
public class MyLibraryProperties {
    private String apiKey;
    // getters/setters
}

// 3. Register in META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports
com.example.MyLibraryAutoConfiguration

// 4. Package as starter
// Users just add dependency and configure:
spring:
  mylibrary:
    api-key: abc123
```

## Performance Impact

Auto-configuration adds ~500ms to startup time for typical application. Spring Boot optimizes by:
- Using condition evaluation caching
- Lazy initialization where possible
- Excluding unnecessary configurations early

**Optimization tip:**
```properties
# Enable lazy initialization
spring.main.lazy-initialization=true  # Reduces startup time by 20-40%
```

## When Auto-Configuration Runs

1. **Application starts** - SpringApplication.run()
2. **Context creation** - ApplicationContext is created
3. **@EnableAutoConfiguration** - AutoConfigurationImportSelector runs
4. **Load configurations** - Reads AutoConfiguration.imports
5. **Evaluate conditions** - Checks @ConditionalOn* annotations
6. **Register beans** - Matching configurations create beans
7. **Post-processing** - BeanPostProcessors run
8. **Application ready** - Context refresh complete

**Critical:** Auto-configuration runs BEFORE your @Configuration classes, so your beans always override auto-configured ones (thanks to @ConditionalOnMissingBean).
